# PLACEHOLDER art for the Infuser. Warlon Mhite intends to redraw these.
#
# The Infuser is a *shaped* block, not a cube with a picture of a tub on it — a hempcrete plinth,
# a waist inset 1px all round, an overhanging rim, an iron pot sunk into the middle and a spout
# bolted to the front. See assets/hempdustry/models/block/infuser.json.
#
# That changes what these files are. Every one of them is drawn as if it were the whole 16x16 face
# of a full block, and the model then cuts the piece it needs out of it at the matching coordinates
# (Minecraft's default per-face UV, the same trick vanilla's cauldron uses). So the bands below are
# not decoration — they land on specific parts of the geometry:
#
#     rows  0-2   the rim            (y 13..16)
#     rows  3-12  the waist          (y 3..13)   <- the inset part, hoops and spout live here
#     rows 13-15  the plinth         (y 0..3)
#
# Consequence worth knowing before editing: the waist's north/south faces sample columns 1-14 and
# its east/west faces sample columns 3-12 of the *same* texture. Anything with vertical structure
# would therefore disagree between the front and the sides. Everything here is banded by row, which
# is why it can't.
#
# The digits 1-5 are sampled from the shipping hempcrete_block.png, so the Infuser sits in the same
# palette as the material it is crafted from and the model can reuse hempcrete_block for its bottom
# face. Hempcrete is a very low-contrast sandy noise, which is correct for a block — vanilla blocks
# average about half the luminance range of items. 6-9 are that same sand pushed darker and lighter,
# for the shadow the rim's overhang casts and the edges that catch the light.

@palette default
. = none
# hempcrete, sampled from hempcrete_block.png
1 = 9C8E58
2 = 9D8F59
3 = 9E9059
4 = 9E905A
5 = 9F915B
# the same sand in shadow (under the overhang, against the ground)
6 = 8A7E4C
7 = 7C7143
# and catching the light (the rim's top edge, the plinth's)
8 = ACA067
9 = B4A870
# the cauldron's ironwork: the pot sunk in the middle, the hoops round the tub, the spout
i = 43423A
j = 5A584D
h = 6E6B5C
u = 32312B
k = 4D4B42
# the one warm pixel on the whole block: butter beading at the spout's mouth
b = C8A64A

# ---- the waist and plinth, seen from the side ----
# Two iron hoops bind the tub (rows 4 and 11), deliberately clear of rows 6-10 where the spout is
# bolted on the front face. Row 3 is the shadow the rim's 1px overhang casts on the waist below it.
@texture block/infuser_side 16x16 type=block
8889988998889989
2331454423422222
4222433221233244
7666666777667766
ijjiijjiijjiijji
3444442144344325
3334524221424332
5325433353444422
3223442243341234
3242224314414222
2421223422423324
ijjiijjiijjiijji
3345434252322222
9888888998899989
3254533444422243
6677777677767766

# ---- the front, which is the side plus a flange ----
# The spout itself is geometry now, so this face only carries the plate it is bolted through. The
# dark centre is the bore: the pipe covers it exactly, so it only ever shows as shadow at a glancing
# angle. The spout is not decoration — the Infuser cannot be drained by a hopper underneath, that
# being where its heat comes from, so it pushes a finished batch out of this face instead. Nothing
# else in the game teaches that, so the block has to.
@texture block/infuser_front 16x16 type=block
8889988998889989
2331454423422222
4222433221233244
7666666777667766
